LICENSE SCOPE — NEW CODE ONLY
============================

The WTFPL version 2 reproduced below applies ONLY to new, original source code
written for this repository, to the extent that the project contributors hold
the rights to that code. This includes the new browser implementation, website
markup and styles, analysis and extraction tools, tests, and packaging code.

THE ORIGINAL TUTTI FRUTTI GAME IS NOT LICENSED UNDER THE WTFPL BY THIS PROJECT.

The WTFPL grant expressly EXCLUDES:

- tuttifrutti.prg, the original Commodore 16 / Plus/4 game by Shaun Southern /
  Mr. Chip Software, published by Mastertronic in 1985;
- original game code, machine instructions, graphics, character sets, maps,
  music, sound data, and any other original material, whether included in the
  PRG or copied, extracted, disassembled, adapted, or embedded elsewhere;
- in particular, the original-derived contents of web/assets.js (assets.js in
  the browser distribution), analysis/disassembly.txt, analysis/level_maps.txt,
  and any reproduced original code or data in the reconstruction artifacts.

New code surrounding or processing excluded material does not relicense that
material. Inclusion in this repository or in an upload archive does not place
the original game or its assets under the WTFPL. Rights in excluded material
remain with their respective rights holders; this project grants no license
on their behalf.

BASIS FOR INCLUDING AND USING THE ORIGINAL GAME
==============================================

The original game and original-derived material are included and used on the
basis of the historical statements in Anthony Guter's interview with Lemon64,
dated June 22, 2002. Guter was Mastertronic's Financial Controller from 1985 to
1991. In the section "Who owns the rights to the classic Mastertronic titles?",
he discusses the original authors' rights and reports that Magnetic Fields had
renounced its claims to its 8-bit titles, concluding:

  "so you can freely use any title produced by Mr. Chip Software."

Source: https://www.lemon64.com/page/anthony-guter-interview

This is the stated basis for this project's use of the original material. It
is separate from the license for the new code. It is not a WTFPL license grant
for the original game, nor a claim that this project owns the original rights.

